      "side": 1,
      "message": "With this change, any existing `HOOK_SYSJUMP` handler that performs I/O waiting for an interrupt (e.g., an I2C transaction that sleeps, or a timer timeout) will now deadlock or fail since interrupts are disabled.\n\nIt is highly recommended to audit existing `HOOK_SYSJUMP` usages across the codebase to ensure none of them rely on interrupts being enabled.\n\nAdditionally, to prevent future regressions, consider adding a unit test that verifies `interrupt_disable_all()` is invoked *before* `hook_notify(HOOK_SYSJUMP)` during a system jump. You could achieve this by tracking the execution order in a test environment:\n\n```c\nstatic int interrupt_disable_called;\n\n/* Mock or wrap interrupt_disable_all */\nvoid interrupt_disable_all(void)\n{\n    interrupt_disable_called \u003d 1;\n    // original implementation or mock...\n}\n\n/* Mock or intercept hook_notify */\nvoid hook_notify(enum hook_type type)\n{\n    if (type \u003d\u003d HOOK_SYSJUMP) {\n        zassert_true(interrupt_disable_called,\n                     \"Interrupts must be disabled before sysjump hooks\");\n    }\n    // ...\n}\n```",
      "isAi": true,
      "revId": "0576d86661f056d0b5630776fd0eab698a668c59",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba"
    },
